  "body": "As a result of download rate decrease and/or playback rate increase, I expect the `readyState` to change from 4 to 3. In that case, according to the spec, there does not seem to be an event that would fire.\r\n\r\nThe closest thing would be the `canplay` event. But according to the spec, it fires only on increase to 3.\r\n\r\nMy use case is a loading indicator that shows to the user that playback is requested but not yet possible OR that an interruption is expected. Basically, it should be shown when `paused === false` and `readyState < 4`. \r\n\r\nSince there is no listening directly `onreadystatechange`, I must resort to listening on some other events:\r\n\r\n- To handle all cases of playback initiation, listen on the `play` event and if `readyState < 4`, show the indicator.\r\n- Show the indicator on the `waiting` event. This would cover `readyState` from 0 to 2.\r\n- Hide the indicator on the `canplaythrough` event.\r\n\r\nWhat event covers `readyState` decreasing from 4 to 3? How can I let my user know that buffering is imminent or expected some time in the future?",
